---

<Layout 
  title="Mock APIs in React Native (2026)" 
  description="How to mock APIs in React Native: MSW for Jest and the simulator, Mirage, record/replay with Mockifyer, Expo setup, and mocking in Detox/Maestro E2E."
  canonicalURL="https://devxtools.dev/guides/mock-api-react-native/"
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <div class="container mx-auto max-w-4xl px-5">
    <article class="prose prose-zinc dark:prose-invert max-w-none py-10">
      <nav class="text-sm text-zinc-500 mb-6">
        <a href="/" class="hover:text-zinc-900 dark:hover:text-white">Home</a>
        /
        <a href="/guides/" class="hover:text-zinc-900 dark:hover:text-white">Guides</a>
        /
        <span class="text-zinc-900 dark:text-white">Mock APIs in React Native</span>
      </nav>

      <div class="not-prose my-8 rounded-lg border border-orange-200 bg-orange-50/60 p-6 dark:border-orange-500/30 dark:bg-orange-500/5">
        <p class="mb-0 text-orange-900/90 dark:text-orange-200/90"><strong>Disclosure:</strong> devxtools.dev is run by Sebastian Gedda, who also builds and maintains <strong>Mockifyer</strong>, one of the tools in this guide. We've tried to keep the comparison fair: MSW and Mirage get at least as much space, and Mockifyer is never ranked first. Mockifyer details come from its README and docs unless we say we tested them.</p>
      </div>

      <h1>How to Mock APIs in React Native</h1>

      <div class="not-prose my-8 rounded-lg border border-indigo-200 bg-indigo-50/60 p-6 dark:border-indigo-500/30 dark:bg-indigo-500/5">
        <p class="mb-0 text-indigo-900/90 dark:text-indigo-200/90"><strong>To mock APIs in React Native, you need different tools for three different places: Jest tests, the running app in a simulator, and end-to-end tests with Detox or Maestro.</strong> This guide covers each one: MSW in Jest (tested), MSW and Expo in the running app (including the MSW 3 change that broke the old setup), Mirage JS, mock servers and the Android 10.0.2.2 gotcha, record/replay, and debugging tools.</p>
      </div>

      <blockquote>
        <p><strong>Tested with</strong> React Native 0.88.0, Jest 30.5.2 (<code>@react-native/jest-preset</code> 0.88.0), React Native Testing Library 14.1.0 and msw 2.15.0 on Node 22.23.3 (2026-10-15). The Jest snippets passed on our machine. We had <strong>no iOS simulator, Android emulator or device</strong> available, so in-app and E2E snippets come from the tools' official docs and are labelled that way.</p>
      </blockquote>

      <h2 id="tldr">TL;DR</h2>

      <p>For React Native, use <strong>MSW</strong> (<code>msw/node</code>) in Jest tests. For the running app, MSW 3's <code>@msw/react-native</code> <strong>isn't on npm yet</strong> (we got a registry 404 on 2026-10-15), so pin msw 2's <code>msw/native</code> or point the app at a <strong>mock server</strong>. Use <strong>Mirage JS</strong> when you want an in-memory fake backend with relationships, a mock server like <strong>Mockoon</strong> or <strong>Prism</strong> for E2E, and a record/replay tool such as <strong>Mockifyer</strong> (built by this site's maintainer) when you want to capture real responses.</p>

      <h2 id="faq">FAQ</h2>

      <h3>How do I mock an API in React Native?</h3>
      <p>In Jest tests, use MSW's <code>setupServer</code> from <code>msw/node</code> with React Native Testing Library. In the running app, either pin msw 2.15 and use <code>msw/native</code> with the URL and TextEncoder polyfills, or point the app's base URL at a mock server such as Prism, Mockoon or WireMock.</p>

      <h3>Does MSW work with React Native and Expo?</h3>
      <p>In Jest, yes: it runs in Node (we tested msw 2.15 with React Native 0.88). In the app, MSW 3 removed <code>msw/native</code> in favour of <code>@msw/react-native</code>, which wasn't published on npm as of 2026-10-15. MSW 2's <code>msw/native</code> with polyfills works with bare React Native and Expo per MSW 2's docs, labelled "use at your own risk".</p>

      <h3>How do I mock fetch in React Native Jest tests?</h3>
      <p>Use MSW so components call the real <code>fetch</code> and handlers return responses. With the React Native Jest preset, allow msw's ESM dependencies to be transformed (transform <code>.mjs</code> and extend <code>transformIgnorePatterns</code>), and <code>await render(...)</code> with RNTL 14. For a one-off, <code>jest.spyOn(global, 'fetch')</code> also works.</p>

